# What is stored here and what is checked out, on every system.
#
# Three tests hold a tracked file to its exact bytes: the MIT licence word for
# word, the import example against the guide that quotes it, and one test that
# reads its own source for the anchors it pins the screen with. A checkout that
# turned those files into CRLF would break all three for a reason that has
# nothing to do with the application, whose own output is the same bytes on
# both platforms (PLAN 14.8.4). So every text file is LF, everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Two files are already not LF and must stay exactly as they are, so they are
# kept out of every conversion rather than normalised into one:
#
#   the CSV example is CRLF because RFC 4180 says a CSV is, and the
#   application writes it that way on both platforms;
#   the Gradle wrapper's batch file is CRLF because Windows' command
#   interpreter reads it a line at a time.
*.csv -text
*.bat -text

# Binary: never examined for line endings, never converted.
*.jar binary
*.xlsx binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
